// wrap-shape-demo — text that follows a figure's silhouette, not just its bounding box.
//
// A wrapped figure narrows the lines beside it by consulting a cutout: a region of the galley the text flows around.
// Until now that region was a plain rectangle — the figure's full bounding box. \wrapfigure[shape] gives the cutout a
// profile instead, a depth-to-inset function, so the running text curves in around a disc and back out again, or
// follows the slope of a wedge. The figure is drawn to match the named silhouette; the engine narrows each line to
// the widest point the shape reaches across that line's band, so the text hugs the outline without ever crossing it.
//
// Captions are left out of the shaped figures on purpose: a shaped cutout treats the whole figure box as that
// silhouette, and a caption riding low in the box would sit where the shape has tapered toward the margin — where the
// wrapping text is allowed back in. Keep captions for rectangular wraps; a shaped figure is the picture alone.

\use{document}
\use{float}

\title {Wrapping text around a shape}
\author {the float package}
\maketitle

\section{A disc}

\wrapfigure[ellipse]{r}{1.6in}{
  \picture width:1.6in height:1.6in {
    \fill{lightblue}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\circle{57.6 57.6 57.6}
  }
}
A disc anchored on the right pushes the text away by its full width only at its widest point, across the middle. Near
the top and the foot of the circle the silhouette curves back toward the margin, and the text follows it in, so the
column has a rounded bite taken out of its right edge rather than a square one. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet,
consectetur adipiscing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim
veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Duis aute irure dolor in
reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat nulla pariatur. Once the lines clear the bottom of the
disc they return to the full measure and run flush to the margin again.

\section{A wedge}

\wrapfigure[triangledown]{l}{1.5in}{
  \picture width:1.5in height:1.5in {
    \fill{lightgreen}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\polygon{0 0 0 108 108 108}
  }
}
A right triangle anchored on the left has its vertical leg on the margin and its hypotenuse sloping down to a point at
the foot. The text to its right follows that slope: the first lines stand well clear of the wide top, and each line
below steps a little closer to the margin as the figure narrows beneath it. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non
proident, sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum. Sed ut perspiciatis unde omnis iste natus
error sit voluptatem accusantium doloremque laudantium, totam rem aperiam, eaque ipsa quae ab illo inventore
veritatis et quasi architecto beatae vitae dicta sunt explicabo. Nemo enim ipsam voluptatem quia voluptas sit.

\section{The plain rectangle still works}

\wrapfigure{r}{1.5in}{
  \picture width:1.5in height:1in {
    \fill{lightyellow}\stroke{black}\linewidth{1pt}\rect{1 1 106 70}
  }
  \vskip 4pt
  \caption{A rectangular wrap, captioned as before.}
}
With no shape given, a wrapped figure is the ordinary rectangle, and a caption inside it numbers and wraps within
the figure column exactly as it always has. Neque porro quisquam est, qui dolorem ipsum quia dolor sit amet,
consectetur, adipisci velit, sed quia non numquam eius modi tempora incidunt ut labore et dolore magnam aliquam
quaerat voluptatem. Ut enim ad minima veniam, quis nostrum exercitationem ullam corporis suscipit laboriosam.
